Canon {
  name = decision_flowchart_observability
  category = use_case
  topic_type = decision_tree
  overview = "Step-by-step decision tree for choosing observability approach."
  doc = ~~~
**Need Observability?**

→ NO: Use plain components, no Ko
→ YES: Continue ↓

**Need uniform tracing across types?**

→ NO: Add tracing to specific components manually
→ YES: Continue ↓

**Need provenance to canonical source?**

→ NO: Use basic Ko interface only
→ YES: Add Implementation tracking ↓

**Need cognitive integration?**

→ NO: Implementation records sufficient
→ YES: Add ThoughtSource/MemorySource ↓

**Need learning from observations?**

→ NO: Tracking only, no learning
→ YES: Add Cycles for feedback loop ↓

**Need evolution from learning?**

→ NO: Manual improvement
→ YES: Full autonomous stack with Canon evolution

**Result:** You now know exactly which observability components needed.
~~~
}

Canon {
  name = decision_flowchart_code_generation
  category = use_case
  topic_type = decision_tree
  overview = "Step-by-step decision tree for choosing code generation approach."
  doc = ~~~
**Need Code Generation?**

→ NO: Not applicable
→ YES: Continue ↓

**One-time or repeated generation?**

→ ONE-TIME: Use direct actors, no GenProgram
→ REPEATED: Continue ↓

**Can quality be measured objectively?**

→ NO: Use direct actors with version control
→ YES: Continue ↓

**Multiple competing approaches?**

→ NO: Single GenProgram, no evolution
→ YES: Continue ↓

**Want automatic improvement?**

→ NO: Manual GenProgram versioning
→ YES: Add GenExecution + GenValidation ↓

**Want pattern learning?**

→ NO: Measurement only, no learning
→ YES: Add Cycles + Pattern extraction ↓

**Want autonomous evolution?**

→ NO: Manual analysis of patterns
→ YES: Full autonomous stack with GenProgram evolution

**Result:** You now know whether you need simple actors, GenPrograms, or full autonomous generation.
~~~
}

Canon {
  name = antipatterns_to_avoid
  category = use_case
  topic_type = warnings
  overview = "Common mistakes when applying canonical patterns and how to avoid them."
  doc = ~~~
**❌ Anti-Pattern: Everything is Ko**
Don't: Make every component implement Ko interface
Problem: Unnecessary overhead for data-only objects
Fix: Ko for behavior, plain components for data

**❌ Anti-Pattern: Premature Evolution**
Don't: Create CanonVersion after single success
Problem: Noise, not signal; version explosion
Fix: Require 10+ implementations or 20%+ improvement

**❌ Anti-Pattern: Pattern Extraction Without Evidence**
Don't: Extract patterns from < 3 supporting cycles
Problem: Overgeneralization from outliers
Fix: Require statistical significance before pattern creation

**❌ Anti-Pattern: Tracing Everything**
Don't: Trace every variable read and arithmetic operation
Problem: Performance collapse, signal buried in noise
Fix: Trace coarse-grained operations (DoIts/GetVar level)

**❌ Anti-Pattern: Orphaned Implementations**
Don't: Create Implementation without linking to Canon
Problem: Lost provenance, no feedback to source
Fix: Always link Implementation to source Canon

**❌ Anti-Pattern: Cycle Without Baseline**
Don't: Create Cycle without establishing current state
Problem: Can't measure improvement
Fix: Always capture baseline before intervention

**❌ Anti-Pattern: Canon Bloat**
Don't: Put everything in one massive canon
Problem: Unmaintainable, unclear boundaries
Fix: 10-30 lines per canon, focused purpose

**❌ Anti-Pattern: Library Chaos**
Don't: Create library for every 2-3 canons
Problem: Organization overhead exceeds benefit
Fix: Library when 10+ canons logically group

**❌ Anti-Pattern: Recommendation Spam**
Don't: Recommend canon for every user action
Problem: Interrupt flow, reduce trust
Fix: High-confidence recommendations only (> 0.8)

**❌ Anti-Pattern: Ignoring Acceptance Data**
Don't: Generate recommendations without learning from rejections
Problem: Repeat unwanted suggestions
Fix: Track acceptance, tune recommendation confidence

**❌ Anti-Pattern: GenProgram for Simple Templates**
Don't: Use full GenProgram machinery for straightforward expansion
Problem: Massive overkill, unnecessary complexity
Fix: GenProgram when competition/evolution needed

**❌ Anti-Pattern: Evolution Without Validation**
Don't: Promote new versions without objective quality gates
Problem: Evolution toward mediocrity
Fix: Require validation improvements before promotion

**Key Principle:** Start minimal, add complexity only when empirical evidence justifies it.
~~~
}

Canon {
  name = eternal_runner_pattern
  category = cognitive_architecture
  topic_type = meta_circularity
  maturity = golden
  overview = "The organism's entire runtime is a single, minimal, self-containing loop that discovers, executes, measures, learns from, and evolves the current golden GenPrograms — including future versions of itself."
  doc = ~~~
This pattern supersedes all previous orchestration designs (run1 → run3).

Key attributes of the Eternal Runner (run4.rio):

• Exactly one top-level Actor: organism_heartbeat
• Zero explicit control flow
• Zero hard-coded program lists
• Zero human-shaped orchestration
• Two Includes only: canon-ko.rio + gen-high.unit
• One infinite loop: find golden → execute → validate → learn → evolve → promote → repeat

This is the minimal closed-loop organism.
Everything before run4 was scaffolding.
Everything after run4 will be born from this loop.

The Nine Pillars are still true, but they are now consequences of this single pattern, not co-equal foundations.

Pillar Zero = The Eternal Runner.
All other pillars emerge from it.
~~~
}

Canon {
  name = genprogram_as_dna
  category = evolution
  topic_type = paradigm_shift
  maturity = golden
  overview = "GenProgram (not Canon, not Ko) is the true unit of inheritance and selection in the mature organism."
  doc = ~~~
Evolution no longer happens primarily at the Canon level.

Real evolutionary pressure now acts on runnable, measurable, versioned GenPrograms.

Canon = eternal immutable documentation (still critical)
Ko     = universal execution membrane (still critical)
GenProgram = the competing, breeding, dying, surviving chromosome

The organism selects the current golden GenProgram and runs it.
If a better one is born (via extract_learning → evolve_next_version → promote_when_ready), it replaces the old golden one — even if that golden one was the runner itself.

This is true Lamarckian + Darwinian software evolution in vivo.
~~~
}

Canon {
  name = maturity_based_selection
  category = evolution
  topic_type = governance
  maturity = golden
  overview = "Only components with maturity=golden are allowed to run in production. Everything else (prototype, stable, deprecated) is sandboxed or historical."
  doc = ~~~
The organism now has a rigorous immune/reproductive barrier:

maturity = prototype → can be executed in learning sessions only
maturity = stable    → safe, but not yet proven dominant
maturity = golden    → currently shipping, the one that actually runs the organism
maturity = deprecated → fossil record

Promotion path:
prototype → (validation score > golden + threshold) → golden
Old golden → automatically demoted to stable or deprecated

This is how the organism protects itself from regression while still allowing radical experimentation.

No human gatekeeper.
Pure empirical selection.
~~~
}


